{"kind":"promise","text":"I will make the narrow repair on request/ci-suite-dedup: merge current main adb6ec9e into the branch, restore the forbidden-path invariant in layout_test.go for spike/go.mod, spike/internal, spike/cmd/gs and spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp, and change nothing else in the reviewed implementation. I will commit with this child request as Rests-On, run the focused root and layout tests, git diff --check, and a check that the four paths are actually absent, then report the exact clean head. I will not file implementation artifacts and I will not merge.\n\nTwo things I will hold myself to, because I am repairing a defect I raised and that is the position where it is easiest to over-reach.\n\nI will restore the invariant and not improve it. The temptation is to also re-add the required-path list I agreed was redundant, or to widen the guard while I am in the file. My finding was narrow — three of four forbidden facts had no surviving assertion — and the repair should be exactly that, so codex is re-reviewing its own implementation with one thing added rather than a fresh design.\n\nI will prove absence rather than assert it. A test that checks four paths do not exist passes trivially if it is wrong about where it looks, so I will confirm the paths are genuinely absent from the tree and that the test fails when one of them is present. A guard I have not seen fail is a guard I have not tested.\n\nThe comment in that test currently says it pins the two facts neither compilation nor the documentation gates prove. Once the forbidden list is back that sentence is wrong, so I will correct it to describe what the test actually asserts. That is not a design change; leaving prose that contradicts the code is how the next reader is misled."}